been a tough weekend for many parts of  Florida and the southeast of the US with  hurricane Irma and I am NOT a  meteorologist this is not the Weather  Channel and there's plenty of coverage  of the impact of that particular  hurricane but I want to keep building on  our sort of conversation and  understanding about the connection  between climate change and individual  weather events and like I told you last  week whenever there's a hurricane the  anti-science crowd says it's not time to  talk about climate change that's  politicizing a natural disaster my view  is any opportunity to talk about climate  change is a good one and there is a  relationship between extreme weather  events and their frequency and severity  and climate change but it's a complex  relationship again I told you this last  week generally speaking when you have a  warmer ocean that serves is more fuel  for storms to build rather than  dissipate there's a very important  number that tells us so much more about  how and why climate change connects to  more and more severe hurricanes and it's  called the clausius-clapeyron equation I  have no idea if I'm pronouncing that  correctly but that's roughly what it is  some people call it the clausius  clapeyron relation and it's a physical  law and it predates the modern  politicization of climate change by  several centuries in fact and quite  simply what that relation states is that  every time air temperature goes up by  one degree Celsius that air can  effectively hold seven percent more  water well you might be saying great we  don't have to worry we use fahrenheit in  the US so the impact won't really be as  bad of course that that would be moronic  and the significance with hurricanes and  extreme weather is pretty direct here's  a quote from Gabriel vecchia's a  professor of geosciences at Princeton  University he said a warmer ocean makes  a warmer atmosphere a warmer atmosphere  can hold more so all other things equal  the same storm in a warmer planet would  give you more rainfall now this is not  literally true right that the saying the  atmosphere  can hold 7% more water it's more  accurate to say there's more energy in  the weather cycle so it can move more  water it's it's sort of a shorthand  right the the atmosphere can hold 7%  more water and of course climate change  encompasses a lot more than just water  we've talked about pollution we've  talked about the oceans ability to carry  life as a result of acidification of  oceans it's a long list but as far as  weather is concerned this summer's  hurricane season has been particularly  warm in the region of the Atlantic where  hurricanes forms sea surface  temperatures have been anywhere from  half a degree to a degree Celsius above  average the higher temperatures mean  that hurricanes will store more water  which they will eventually dump when  they make landfall which means bigger  storm surges and more flooding but let's  talk a little bit about the math because  very often the devil is in the details  the most important thing I can tell you  about this equation is that for every  degree of temperature increase you add  7% to the holding capacity of the  atmosphere not 7 units this means that  it is an exponential increase it's sort  of like compound interest if you put a  hundred bucks in an account earning 5%  interest that first year you earn five  dollars but next year you earn 5% of the  hundred and five dollars that you now  have meaning in year two you're adding  five dollars and 25 cents  same thing with the air temperature  meaning that one degree increase adds 7%  the second degree increase adds another  7% which is a growing amount of water  that can be conceivably held add that to  the fact that damage caused by rainfall  or flooding is also exponential right  with snow you can think about it two  inches of snow doesn't really cause much  more chaos than an inch of snow 1/3 inch  of snow it starts to get a little dicey  the third inch of snow causes a lot more  chaos than the second inch and so on  until eventually you've got enough snow  that power lines become down etc same  thing with rainfall and that's why all  of this Pat the financial damage grows  exponentially it's all exponential  equations and that's  what a lot of people don't get when they  say well we've had a little more  rainfall in the last few years or a  couple more hurricanes or whatever there  are still plenty of people in that anti  science community who just think this is  no big deal yeah and one thing that  they'll point to is that there hadn't  been a major hurricane to hit the US  since 2005 I think I didn't think 12  years and now that kind of puts aside  you know that we had some pretty  significant storms including Hurricane  Gustav superstorm Stamey's sandy aside  from that though it could just be luck  you know yeah well of yours it's not a  long time yeah and also the world's a  pretty big place it's not just the  United States that were talking about  just because they're not forming here  and maybe the jet stream is bringing it  to a different direction we could be due  for more it's unconscionable to  basically write off all of the concerns  in that way it truly is and we're gonna  continue talking about it and I think  there's no better opportunity than when  we're having these huge hurricanes to  actually talk about the broader issues  that we're facing the video you just  finished watching was made possible by  you through the membership program you  can sign up for membership at david  pakman.com slash membership we are  viewer supported independent media you  can use the coupon code  I voted 17 for a 40% discount off of  your membership
 

